\begin{abstract}
We present a non-asymptotic lattice Boltzmann (LB) inverse kinetic theory for the incompressible Navier--Stokes equations. In contrast with conventional and entropic lattice Boltzmann methods, the formulation does not rely on a small-Knudsen-number expansion, a low-Mach-number Chapman--Enskog closure, or an entropy-minimization principle to recover the target fluid equations. Instead, the discrete kinetic model is constructed so that the prescribed macroscopic fields are exact moments of a finite set of kinetic populations, and the hydrodynamic equations are obtained identically at the kinetic level. This provides a direct characterization of the non-equilibrium part of the distribution function, including its role in generating viscous stresses and pressure corrections, without invoking asymptotic truncation.

Particular attention is given to boundary behavior. The inverse construction permits boundary data for velocity and pressure to be imposed consistently at the level of the kinetic populations, reducing ambiguity in the treatment of incoming and outgoing discrete velocities and clarifying the relation between kinetic boundary conditions and macroscopic no-slip or prescribed-traction constraints. The resulting framework also yields explicit accuracy estimates separating discretization error, quadrature error, and kinetic closure error. These estimates show that, under appropriate regularity assumptions, the method reproduces the target continuum dynamics with controlled non-asymptotic accuracy, rather than only in a formal hydrodynamic limit. The theory therefore offers a complementary perspective to entropic LB schemes: while entropic methods enhance nonlinear stability through an entropy functional and adaptive relaxation, the present inverse kinetic approach emphasizes exact moment realization, explicit non-equilibrium structure, and rigorous consistency with the prescribed macroscopic equations.
\end{abstract}
